diff --git a/docs/spec/aprs.md b/docs/spec/aprs.md
index d543233..4b18926 100644
--- a/docs/spec/aprs.md
+++ b/docs/spec/aprs.md
@@ -211,6 +211,19 @@ says whether the path runs.
 When a retune takes the receiver off the APRS frequency or out of FM, the radio task releases the APRS audio path
 before retuning. This lets a digital modem take the receive audio copy and restarts APRS decoding when FM returns.
 
+In dual watch with a single receiver, two consecutive HDLC flags on either Bell 202 demodulator path hold the current
+slot, including during a peek in the other slot's reply window. The FM speaker is muted during detected packet
+activity. The watch resumes after 75 ms without activity, or another slot gets a turn after 3 s of continuous activity
+even if the squelch is still open. Monitor and manual PTT retain priority. An APRS transmission waits during the hold.
+Reception still requires the receiver to catch enough of the preamble to acquire the packet.
+
+`aprsRadioTest` and `aprsRadioMdcTest` exercise the radio state machine with Bell 202 PCM in 256-sample blocks and
+compare accepted AX.25 frames and station entries. On a DM-32UV, repeat with a packet source on the configured APRS
+frequency and Dual Watch off, FM + APRS, and DMR + APRS, selecting each slot in turn. Compare
+`aprs.rx_listening`, `aprs.rx_bits`, `aprs.rx_start_fails`, `aprs.rx_fcs_errors`, and `aprs.rx_frames`. Check the
+speaker during bursts, voice reception on the other slot, and PTT on the selected slot. The host tests simulate the
+capture cadence. The RF tuning, hardware audio copy, and audible speaker behavior need that bench test.
+
 The receive setting and messaging default to on when the radio can receive (`aprs_can_receive()`: the build has
 `CONFIG_APRS_RX` and one of the two paths exists).
 
